    "result_text": "The full test suite passes \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it's green. The compilation of the constructor change succeeded as part of the build.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change requires emitting an audit line via the `AUDIT` logger on successful owner creation, containing the owner id, customerCode, and registrationDate.\n\nI implemented this by keeping responsibilities cleanly separated:\n\n1. **New `OwnerAuditLogger` component** (`rest/controller/OwnerAuditLogger.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility bean that owns the `AUDIT` logger and knows how to format the audit line for a created owner. This mirrors the existing helper convention in that package (normalizers, validators).\n\n2. **Wired into `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected via the constructor and invoked right after `clinicService.saveOwner(owner)` returns, which is the exact \"successful create\" point where the owner's generated id and customerCode plus the resolved registrationDate are all populated.\n\nThe audit line produced looks like:\n```\nAUDIT - Owner created: id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-21\n```\n\nwhich satisfies the test assertion that a single AUDIT message contains all three values. I deliberately avoided putting the logging inside `saveOwner` (which is also used by update and lacks controller-level \"create success\" semantics) or inlining a logger into the controller, keeping each unit focused.",
